{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"Oxford-style debates are a competitive format where the winner is determined by audience votes.","source_span":"a competitive format where the winner is determined by audience votes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"Winners tend to make better use of the debate's interactive component than losers, by actively pursuing opponents' points rather than promoting their own ideas.","source_span":"winners tend to make better use of a debate's interactive component than losers, by actively pursuing their opponents' points rather than promoting their own ideas over the course of the conversation"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"The dataset consists of transcripts of all debates held by IQ2 in the US from September 2006 to September 2015, totaling 108 debates.","source_span":"transcripts of all debates held by IQ2 in the US from September 2006 up to September 2015; in total, there are 108 debates"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"On average, 12801 words are uttered in 117 turns by members of either side per debate.","source_span":"on average, 12801 words are uttered in 117 turns by members of either side per debate"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"In the introduction round, each debater is given 7 minutes to lay out their main points.","source_span":"each debater is given 7 minutes to lay out their main points"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"The discussion round lasts around 30 minutes and is highly interactive.","source_span":"This round lasts around 30 minutes and is highly interactive"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"In the conclusion round, each debater is given 2 minutes to make final remarks.","source_span":"in the conclusion, each debater is given 2 minutes to make final remarks"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"A side wins the debate if the difference between the percentage of votes it receives post- and pre-debate (the 'delta') is greater than that of the other side's.","source_span":"A side wins the debate if the difference between the percentage of votes they receive post- and pre-debate (the “delta”) is greater than that of the other side's."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"For 30% of the debates, the difference between the winning and losing sides' deltas is less than 10%.","source_span":"for 30% of the debates, the difference between the winning and losing sides' deltas is less than 10%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"Winning teams tend to receive more laughter during the discussion round and more applause during the conclusion round.","source_span":"winning teams tend to receive more laughter during the discussion ( INLINEFORM0 ) and more applause during the conclusion"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"In the example debate 'Millennials don't stand a chance', the For side won with a delta of 20% of the votes, compared to the Against side which gained only 5%.","source_span":"The For side won the debate with a delta of 20% of the votes, compared to the Against side which only gained 5%."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","statement":"Each IQ2 debate consists of two opposing teams of two or three experts on the motion's topic, along with a moderator.","source_span":"two opposing teams—one for the motion and one against—of two or three experts in the topic of the particular motion, along with a moderator"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What time period does the Intelligence Squared (IQ2) debate dataset cover, and how many debates does it contain?","answer":"The dataset covers all IQ2 debates held in the US from September 2006 to September 2015, totaling 108 debates."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[4,5,6],"question":"How much time is each debater given in the introduction, discussion, and conclusion rounds of an IQ2 debate?","answer":"Each debater gets 7 minutes for the introduction, the discussion round lasts around 30 minutes, and the conclusion gives each debater 2 minutes for final remarks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How is the winning side of an Oxford-style IQ2 debate determined?","answer":"A side wins if the difference between its post-debate and pre-debate vote percentages (the 'delta') is greater than that of the opposing side."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[3],"question":"On average, how many words and turns occur in an IQ2 debate?","answer":"On average, 12801 words are uttered across 117 turns by members of either side per debate."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[10],"question":"In the 'Millennials don't stand a chance' example debate, what were the vote deltas for the For and Against sides?","answer":"The For side won with a delta of 20% of the votes, while the Against side gained only 5%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[8],"question":"For what percentage of IQ2 debates is the gap between the winning and losing sides' vote deltas less than 10%?","answer":"For 30% of the debates, the difference between the winning and losing sides' deltas is less than 10%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0031","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Do winning and losing debate teams receive different audience reactions during the discussion and conclusion rounds?","answer":"Yes, winning teams tend to receive more laughter during the discussion round and more applause during the conclusion round."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The evaluation data consists of comparable corpora of Russian and Ukrainian academic texts, where topics correspond to academic fields.","source_span":"Our data is a collection of Russian and Ukrainian academic texts, for which topics are their academic fields."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The method trains Continuous Bag-of-Words models on monolingual corpora for each language, then learns a linear transformation matrix from one language's vectors to the other's using a small bilingual dictionary.","source_span":"we train Continuous Bag-of-Words models BIBREF0 on large comparable monolingual corpora for two languages... a linear transformation matrix from vectors of language A to vectors of language B is learned, using a small bilingual dictionary as training data"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The 'translated semantic fingerprints' method achieves almost exact clustering by document topic, with only 5% of incorrect assignments.","source_span":"allow almost exact clustering according to document topics, with only 5% of incorrect assignments"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The proposed method significantly outperforms both a naive bag-of-words baseline and an 'orthographic translation' method based on Damerau-Levenshtein distance enriched with dictionary mappings.","source_span":"It significantly outperforms both naive bag-of-words baseline and the not-so-naive method of `orthographic translation' based on Damerau-Levenshtein distance, even enriched with dictionary mappings"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The proposed method does not require large parallel corpora or a ready-made statistical machine translation model.","source_span":"it does not require large parallel corpora or a ready-made statistical machine translation model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The idea of learning a linear transformation matrix between language vector spaces was first proposed in BIBREF4, applied to lexical translation between English, Spanish, Czech and Vietnamese.","source_span":"This idea was first proposed in BIBREF4, who applied it to lexical translation between English, Spanish, Czech and Vietnamese."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"BIBREF2's joint multi-lingual neural embedding models require a large word-aligned parallel corpus.","source_span":"BIBREF2 proposed training joint multi-lingual neural embedding models... it demands a large word-aligned parallel corpus"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The Trans-gram approach (BIBREF3) needs sentence-aligned corpora in the size of millions of paired sentences.","source_span":"it still needs sentence-aligned corpora to train on (in the size of millions of paired sentences)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","statement":"The approach in this paper requires only comparable monolingual corpora and bilingual dictionaries in the size of several thousand word pairs.","source_span":"The approach described in this paper takes as an input only comparable monolingual corpora and bilingual dictionaries in the size of several thousand word pairs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What languages does this paper's document clustering method target, and how does it build language-independent representations?","answer":"It targets Russian and Ukrainian academic texts, building representations by training Continuous Bag-of-Words models on monolingual corpora for each language and learning a linear transformation matrix between them using a small bilingual dictionary."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What error rate did the 'translated semantic fingerprints' method achieve when clustering Russian and Ukrainian academic texts by topic?","answer":"It achieved almost exact clustering with only 5% of incorrect assignments."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which two baseline methods does the proposed clustering approach outperform?","answer":"It outperforms a naive bag-of-words baseline and an 'orthographic translation' method based on Damerau-Levenshtein distance, even when the latter is enriched with dictionary mappings."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How large a bilingual dictionary does the proposed method require to learn the language transformation?","answer":"It requires bilingual dictionaries of only several thousand word pairs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which languages did BIBREF4 originally apply the linear transformation matrix idea to for lexical translation?","answer":"BIBREF4 applied it to lexical translation between English, Spanish, Czech, and Vietnamese."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Why is the Trans-gram approach (BIBREF3) impractical for under-represented language pairs?","answer":"Because it needs sentence-aligned corpora on the order of millions of paired sentences, which are often scarce for under-represented languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0032","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Does the proposed clustering method require large parallel corpora or a machine translation system?","answer":"No, it does not require large parallel corpora or a ready-made statistical machine translation model."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Data was collected from a 10% uniform sample of Twitter posts made during 2013, via the Gardenhose API.","source_span":"Data was collected from a 10% uniform sample of Twitter posts made during 2013, specifically the Gardenhose API."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Tweets are short posts limited to 140 characters.","source_span":"short posts called tweets which are limited to 140 characters"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Retweets were not considered in the study.","source_span":"Retweets, where users repost a tweet to spread its content, were not considered."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Only tweets with a user-reported language of 'English' that also matched more English stopwords than any other language's stopwords (per NLTK's database) were kept.","source_span":"we kept only tweets with a user-reported language of `English' and, as a second constraint, individual tweets needed to match more English stopwords than any other language's set of stopwords. Stopwords considered for each language were determined using NLTK's database"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Causal documents were chosen to contain exactly one occurrence of the exact unigrams 'caused', 'causing', or 'causes'.","source_span":"Causal documents were chosen to contain one occurrence only of the exact unigrams: `caused', `causing', or `causes'."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"The word 'cause' was excluded from selection criteria because it is popularly used as a contraction for 'because'.","source_span":"The word `cause' was not included due to its use as a popular contraction for `because'."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Documents containing bidirectional words ('associate', 'relate', 'connect', 'correlate', and their stems) were excluded from analysis.","source_span":"Documents that contain bidirectional words (`associate', `relate', `connect', `correlate', and any of their stems) were also not selected for analysis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Causal statements tend to be more negative in sentiment than control statements.","source_span":"tend to be more negative in sentiment than controls"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","statement":"Causal statements made online tend to focus on news and current events, medicine and health, or interpersonal relationships, as shown by topic models.","source_span":"Causal statements made online tend to focus on news and current events, medicine and health, or interpersonal relationships, as shown by topic models."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What data source and sampling method were used to build the causal-statement Twitter corpus?","answer":"The corpus was built from a 10% uniform sample of Twitter posts made during 2013, collected via the Gardenhose API."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which exact unigrams were used to identify causal documents in the Twitter corpus?","answer":"Causal documents were chosen to contain exactly one occurrence of 'caused', 'causing', or 'causes'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why was the word 'cause' excluded when selecting causal documents?","answer":"It was excluded because 'cause' is popularly used as a contraction for 'because', not as a causal indicator."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What kind of documents were excluded from the causal analysis due to bidirectionality?","answer":"Documents containing bidirectional words like 'associate', 'relate', 'connect', 'correlate', or their stems were excluded."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How does the sentiment of causal statements compare to control statements on Twitter?","answer":"Causal statements tend to be more negative in sentiment than control statements."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are the three main topic areas that causal statements on Twitter tend to focus on?","answer":"They tend to focus on news and current events, medicine and health, or interpersonal relationships."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0033","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"Were retweets included in the Twitter causal-statement study, and how was the English-language filter applied?","answer":"Retweets were not considered; tweets were kept only if user-reported as English and if they matched more English stopwords than stopwords of any other language, per NLTK's database."}
